<BaseLayout title="The Second Hand — I AM ROBIN" description="Weekly short plays on human–AI coevolution."><main><a href="/binary/">← Binary</a><h1>The Second Hand</h1><p>Weekly short plays about the habits people and machines teach each other.</p><nav aria-label="Episodes"><a href="#yellow-cup">November 21, 2026 · The Yellow Cup</a></nav>
<article id="yellow-cup"><h2>The Yellow Cup</h2><p><i>A short play. The people and events are fictional.</i></p>
<p>In a radio studio, Nell is recording the last item for her father's oral history. A yellow cup sits on the mixing desk. Kit, the archive assistant, has left her eleven seconds of silence to fill.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> Dad brought it home from the docks on my seventh birthday.</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> Would you like the sound of the spoon again?</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> There was a spoon?</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> You asked me to add one. Take four.</p>
<p>Three bright taps against china. Nell smiles before she can stop herself.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> Exactly how he stirred his tea.</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> You did those taps with a house key.</p>
<p>She turns the cup over. Underneath, in her sister’s block capitals, it says PROP DEPT. £2.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> Did I ever tell you what colour his was?</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> On Monday you said you wished you could remember. On Wednesday you sent me a photograph of this one. I wrote “his yellow cup” on the file.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> And on Thursday?</p>
<p><b>KIT</b> You read my caption aloud. That’s the recording I used to confirm the colour.</p>
<p>The red light comes on. Nell puts down the cup.</p>
<p><b>NELL</b> My father used to stir his tea long after the sugar had dissolved. I can still hear him doing it.</p>
<p>She reaches for the key. Then she leaves it on the desk. For the rest of the eleven seconds, the listener hears the room.</p>
<aside><h3>After the curtain</h3><p>Edward Ruoyang Shi asks when shared, revisable memory might make a person and an AI a cognitive system. Experiments he cites show agents retrieving stored events to guide later actions. A shared mind remains a philosophical possibility. Nell and Kit suggest a rival explanation for their agreement. Each can hear the other repeating the same mistake.</p><p>For a long-lived archive, keep the original recording beside every inference. When a speaker reads a machine-written caption back into the record, carry its source forward. Two voices can still amount to one witness.</p><p>Edward Ruoyang Shi, September 18, 2026. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1768785">From evolutionary individuality to cognitive integration: a cross-modular perspective on human–AI coevolution</a>.</p></aside></article></main></BaseLayout>
